WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.290
Log levels should communicate operational meaning.

2
00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.961
Debug events help developers inspect detailed behavior.

3
00:00:04.961 --> 00:00:07.633
Info events record normal, meaningful state changes.

4
00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:10.686
Warnings identify unusual conditions that may need attention.

5
00:00:10.686 --> 00:00:15.647
Error events represent failed operations, and critical-style events mark urgent system impact.

6
00:00:15.647 --> 00:00:19.845
The level should not be chosen only by convenience or habit.

7
00:00:19.845 --> 00:00:23.280
If every event is an error, operators lose signal.

8
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.242
If important failures are buried at a verbose level, responders may miss them.

9
00:00:28.242 --> 00:00:31.295
A level policy helps teams interpret logs consistently.

10
00:00:31.295 --> 00:00:34.348
Events also need stable names and component context.

11
00:00:34.348 --> 00:00:40.073
A clear event name, component, operation, and outcome make logs easier to query and review.

12
00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:45.416
Stable fields are better than slightly different free-text messages scattered across the codebase.

13
00:00:45.416 --> 00:00:46.942
Safe correlation is valuable.

14
00:00:46.942 --> 00:00:54.957
Request IDs, correlation IDs, operation IDs, tenant-safe identifiers, or non-sensitive object IDs can connect related events without revealing raw

15
00:00:54.957 --> 00:00:55.720
sensitive data.

16
00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.681
The identifier should be designed for investigation, not copied blindly from untrusted input.

17
00:01:00.681 --> 00:01:04.498
Avoid vague messages such as operation failed with no context.

18
00:01:04.498 --> 00:01:08.696
Also avoid noisy repetition that floods storage and hides important events.

19
00:01:08.696 --> 00:01:13.657
Repeated failures may need rate limiting, aggregation, or a summary event with counters.

20
00:01:13.657 --> 00:01:16.710
The goal is useful context without over-collection.

21
00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:25.107
A maintainer should be able to understand what happened and where to investigate next, while sensitive data, raw inputs, and unnecessary internals

22
00:01:25.107 --> 00:01:27.015
remain out of the record.

23
00:01:27.015 --> 00:01:29.686
Context should also be stable across releases.

24
00:01:29.686 --> 00:01:35.793
If field names, event names, or status values change casually, dashboards and incident playbooks lose reliability.

25
00:01:35.793 --> 00:01:39.609
Treat the event schema as part of the operational interface.

26
00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:46.860
When a message needs more detail, prefer adding a reviewed structured field over stuffing more prose into the text.

27
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.440
That keeps the event searchable and makes future privacy review much easier.
